      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Dracon's Laws</strong><br>\nDracon wrote down the laws for the first time around 621 BCE. His laws were known for their harshness; hence, the term 'draconian' came to be associated with severe punishment. For many offenses, the penalty was death, indicating a rigid approach to maintaining public order.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Impact on Society</strong><br>\nDracon's laws were a significant step forward for Athenian society. They provided a more predictable and consistent basis for legal judgments. The written codes allowed citizens to become aware of the laws and understand the consequences of their actions, thereby reducing arbitrariness in punishments.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Public Reaction</strong><br>\nWhile Dracon's laws brought some order, the severe penalties for even minor offenses caused much dissent. The public's reaction was mixed, as some viewed the new system as too punitive. Over time, the outcry led to demands for reforms, which were later taken up by other lawmakers.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
